{
  "id": "COMPOUND:42484 - PROTEIN:5028",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which 7,4',7'',4'''-O-Methyl-Amentoflavone interacts with COLO-824, through a series of molecular targets that ultimately converge on the same cellular pathway. This compound is implicated in the regulation of guanylate cyclase soluble subunit beta-1, which is a key component of the Gs protein-coupled receptor signaling pathway. This target is also engaged by two distinct compounds, (3R)-3-hydroxy-12-[(2R,5R,6S)-5-hydroxy-6-methylpiperidin-2-yl]dodecanoic acid and Quiquesetinerviusin B, both of which are shown to interact with COLO-824. The shared involvement of guanylate cyclase soluble subunit beta-1 in these interactions suggests a common mechanism of action, possibly involving the modulation of intracellular signaling through Gs-coupled receptors. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust and reliable relationship, supported by multiple independent targets and pathways. The mechanism appears to be centered on the regulation of cellular signaling through the Gs-coupled receptor pathway, with the compound acting as a modulator of this process.",
